00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:11.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a guide to grammar the infographics  [Music]  introduction  english is a challenging language to  master even for native speakers  why well for starters  it's been spiced by many dialects and  regional variations  [Music]  adjectives  an adjective modifies a noun or a  pronoun to give more information about a  person place or think verbs  a verb is a word that indicates an  action or a state of being  it tells the story of the sentence  [Music]  a verb tenses  verb tenses relate action to time  telling us whether something has  occurred in the past is happening right  now or will take place in the future  regular versus irregular verbs  exceptions to the rule as if learning  about tenses wasn't complicated enough  the english language stumps learners  with irregular verbs in fact some of our  most common verbs  are irregular pronouns a pronoun is a  word that takes the place of a noun to  add variety and avoid a repetition  prepositions and prepositional phrases  as you can see from the infographic  these are also known as linking words  a preposition connects a noun or a  pronoun to another word in the sentence  to show the relationship between the two  conjunctions or connectors  think of conjunctions as words that join  two parts of a sentence together  interjections  expressing emotion  what really you need help on  interjections  no way  sentence functions a sentence is made up  of one or more words and expresses a  complete thought in a statement question  request command or  exclamation subject and predicates  major parts of a sentence  sentences can be very short or very long  at a minimum though they must contain a  subject and a predicate how do we make  smooth sentences subject verb agreement  there is only one rule about  subject-verb agreement you must make  verbs agree with their subject in number  and in person of course this is english  so this rule can be tricky to follow  compound subjects compound subjects  consist of two or more individual nouns  or noun phrases connected by and or or  to form a single longer noun phrase  complements  making sense we have five kinds of  compliments  while some sentences contain only a  subject and a verb others use more words  to make things clearer tell a story or  ask a question these additional sentence  components are called complements  phrases a phrase is a group of words  that adds detail to a sentence but  doesn't have a subject and a verb  phrases can be part of sentences but  cannot stand alone adding detail we have  five types of phrases clauses  a clause is a phrase with a subject and  a verb and any complements that verb  requires  depending on the type it may or may not  be able to stand alone as a sentence we  have different kind of clauses  we'll get into that later  restrictive clauses and non-restrictive  clauses categorizing clauses is not easy  clauses can also be categorized by  whether or not they are necessary to the  meaning of a sentence after all  sometimes we need more detail and  sometimes we don't  whereas non-restrictive clauses could be  omitted from a sentence without changing  its meaning parallelism and logical  sentences  sometimes you must organize related  thoughts show that actions are  sequential or build to a climax and make  a point these cases call for parallelism  which means using the same grammatical  structure for all the similar parts of a  sentence capitalization using upper case  when you capitalize you write a word  with the first letter in uppercase and  the remaining letters in lowercase  ending a sentence when ending a sentence  you have three choices a period a  question mark or an exclamation point  use a period when your sentence states a  fact or makes a command  apostrophes and contractions the  apostrophe can cause confusion but it  doesn't have to three basic situations  require an apostrophe making a  contraction indicating possession or  forming a plural case  commas  creating a pause the comma creates a  slight pause in a sentence and helps  readers understand how items relate to  one another  quotation marks show which words are  yours and which belong to someone else  direct quotations most commonly  quotation marks show readers the exact  word someone said in the exact order  they were said  collins and sami collins  proper punctuation is very important  semicolon indicates a pause that is more  significant than a comma but less  significant than a period but most often  they connect two complete thoughts that  could be two separate sentences hyphens  m dashes and n dashes  word dividers hyphens and dashes  separate words and thoughts  a hyphen is shorter while n and m dashes  are longer parenthesis set off extra  information material that may not be  necessary but can be helpful  we use parentheses to set off a part of  a sentence  for dates series political affiliation  and alternate endings brackets square  brackets are most commonly found in  dictionaries where they enclose the  history of the word being defined other  than this usage there are a few key  places where you'll find them  parentheses and brackets always come in  pairs with an opening mark and a closing  mark bold italics and underlining all  emphasize words although you should use  these elements sparingly here's when to  use them in titles the names of movies  books poem collections plays oprah's tv  shows works of art magazines newspapers  ships  ellipsis an ellipsis is a punctuation  mark comprising three dots or periods  with no space between them ellipses have  two main functions to signal word  emission from a quoted passage or to  express mood  [Music]  the slash has four main uses in writing  one separates portions of the address in  a url two stands in for or  as in students may bring their mother  slash fathers to the open house  [Music]  abbreviations and acronyms an  abbreviation is a series of letters that  serves as a shortened version of a  longer word you still read or speak the  full word an acronym is a new word  formed by the initial letters of the  word it comprises identifying your  purpose and audience content and  connection knowing your specific  audience will guide what you write and  the words you use one determine and  purpose two identify your audience three  consider content four decide on  definitions five create connection and  six call to action active and passive  voice sentences with action verbs and  direct objects can be written in active  voice or passive voice understand the  difference between the two  [Music]  transitions making writing sound natural  we use these to show sequence or explain  steps to indicate cause and effect to  compare the contracts to show addition  or emphasis to indicate time to  generalize to show place paragraph  structure i'm guessing this is one of  the most boring classes you've ever had  in english a paragraph is a group of  sentences united by a single topic or  point knowing how to construct a  paragraph will help you write coherently  and persuasively fixing repetition and  introducing variety  making writing sparkle clarity is  important in writing but so is variety  keep your readers interested by avoiding  repetition in sentence length and word  choice foreign words and phrases english  is a language of borrowed words with  influences from german french and many  other tongues when using foreign words  and phrases how do you write them  improving writing there are four tips to  improve our writing like any skill your  writing ability improves over time with  practice here are a few tips that can  help  [Music]  cliches generalizations platitudes and  shotgun words of caution tips of  avoiding these traps there's much magic  in knowing the right words to use as in  knowing which ones to avoid  [Music]  commonly confused words  learn the difference to avoid mistakes  homophones are pronounced the same way  but have very different meanings for  example i and i even native english  speakers struggle with some words and  expressions  who and whom  which pronoun if you struggle to decide  whether to use who or whom in writing  and speech just remember who is always  the subject of the verb in the sentence  while whom is the object  more common mistakes these words can  also cause confusion  [Music]  commonly misused phrases  language traps confusing phrases knowing  the proper phrase and its spelling can  go a long way toward improving your  communication whether it's professional  or personal  modifier problems in the wrong place at  the wrong time when you're using  modifiers the words should be as close  as possible to whatever they describe or  elaborate on otherwise mistakes can  happen run on sentences and wordiness  too many words  trust me i know exactly what you're  saying contrary to their name rhino  sentences aren't necessarily long  fuzzy grammar  when accuracy counts throughout life  you'll encounter situations in which  total grammatical accuracy  is in your best interest  these instances include formal writing  assignments such as school papers  exams and published work and other  high-stakes situations such as cover  letters and job applications

